WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.763
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.763 --> 00:00:11.751
I want you to take a moment and
picture the person who

00:00:11.751 --> 00:00:16.170
gave you your first big
career opportunity.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:21.309
The person who took a chance on you to
do something you'd never done before.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.490
Maybe before you were really ready.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:29.145
Do you remember their name?

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:32.843
What they looked like?

00:00:34.655 --> 00:00:36.548
What that meant for you?

00:00:41.418 --> 00:00:46.450
So, I don't know who you're picturing,
what their name is or what they look like.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.385
But I do know something
important Important about them.

00:00:50.825 --> 00:00:56.850
In some meaningful way
they changed your life.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.780
Now often when we talk about people
like this we call them mentors.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.947
And we know that mentors
are people who give us advice,

00:01:05.947 --> 00:01:09.960
share perspective, teach us new skills.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.055
And we're told that early in our career we
should be constantly scanning our working

00:01:14.055 --> 00:01:14.815
environment.

00:01:14.815 --> 00:01:16.845
Always on the hunt for that next mentor.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:25.440
But of everyone who mentors you
has the power to help you move up.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.340
To get to that next level to advocate for
you when it really counts.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.100
We need a different word for that.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.200
We'll call that sponsorship.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.570
And while I believe that mentors matter,
we need sponsors to be really successful.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.703
So, what is sponsorship?

00:01:46.703 --> 00:01:49.920
A sponsor sees a potential in you.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.870
They give you access to
the kinds of experiences and

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:57.680
relationships that they already have, that
you may not be able to find on your own.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.216
And finally, they'll put their own
reputation on the line to advocate for

00:02:02.216 --> 00:02:03.880
your commotion.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.300
Whether it's to greater responsibility or
visibility.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:13.141
Now my guess is that each of you could
probably tell me right now specifically

00:02:13.141 --> 00:02:14.840
what your sponsor did for you.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:20.820
But, sponsorship as a skill
set that is generalizable.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.250
It matters in every profession.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.227
So to picture this, I want you to
picture a professional hero of mine.

00:02:27.227 --> 00:02:31.305
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yoda.

00:02:31.305 --> 00:02:35.679
Now, I loved Star Wars as a kid and
I love it even more now because Yoda,

00:02:35.679 --> 00:02:41.350
it turns out, is one of the greatest
sponsors of all time, and here's why.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:42.590
When we first meet him,

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:47.550
Luke Skywalker looks a lot more like
a farm hand then a Jedi Master, but

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:53.410
Yoda sees through that to the potential
in him and decides to sponsor him.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.257
Next, he creates opportunities for

00:02:55.257 --> 00:02:58.840
Luke to learn the kinds of skills
he'll need to be successful as a Jedi.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.230
Now what's important about this,
is that he's not just giving Luke advise.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:08.420
He's giving him access to the kinds of
experiences that Luke wouldn't have

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:09.970
known how to find on his own.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.830
And finally, and if you haven't already
been taking this metaphor with a grain of

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:19.350
salt you could start now,
Yoda's dying act is to promote Luke.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.790
He makes him the last of the living Jedi,
and

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:25.120
that former farm hand goes
on to save the Galaxy.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:28.860
So, at this point,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.410
you might think that this talk is about
the importance of finding your Yoda.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.844
Someone to advocate for you and
help you achieve great things.

00:03:36.844 --> 00:03:42.328
But actually this is a talk about
the importance of being a Yoda and

00:03:42.328 --> 00:03:45.918
how to discover that greatness in others,

00:03:45.918 --> 00:03:49.927
and there's three
important reasons to do so.

00:03:49.927 --> 00:03:52.619
First, and this might be obvious, but

00:03:52.619 --> 00:03:57.770
sponsorship is a way to have a measurable
impact on somebody else's life.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.960
Think back to the person you
pictured at the beginning.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.735
You know what a powerful impact
that sponsorship had for you.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.515
But what if you had never met that person?

00:04:10.595 --> 00:04:15.845
Or, what if you had, but they just hadn't
chosen you to take that chance on?

00:04:17.435 --> 00:04:19.433
Where would you be now?

00:04:21.823 --> 00:04:27.590
That gap between what could have been and
what is is tangible to you.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:32.190
Although, perhaps hard to
measure except looking back.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.704
So if you want to change lives,

00:04:34.704 --> 00:04:39.165
sponsorship is a way to fill that gap for
someone else.

00:04:39.165 --> 00:04:43.280
Now, second, and
this is perhaps less intuitive.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.390
Sponsorship turns out to be good for
the sponsor.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.080
Imagine having a bench
of people who feel about

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.310
you the way you feel about
the person you pictured.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:54.330
What could you get done?

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:55.815
And this isn't fluffy stuff.

00:04:55.815 --> 00:05:01.735
A delight study found that 70% of people
who self identify as sponsors reported

00:05:01.735 --> 00:05:07.080
faster progression in their own careers,
and this makes sense, right?

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.680
Because while earlier in our
careers we're rewarded for

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.740
being strong individual producers.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:17.660
There comes a time when the things we're
asked to achieve, we can't do on our own.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:21.174
And that's when having a bench counts.

00:05:21.174 --> 00:05:24.510
And there's something powerful about
filling that bench with people who

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.430
are early in their own careers.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:30.580
There is a saying in Silicon Valley that
there's two kinds of investors here.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:34.707
People who invested in the Facebook early,
and people who didn't but wish they did.

00:05:34.707 --> 00:05:37.326
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now I can't tell you how to

00:05:37.326 --> 00:05:38.992
invest your money.

00:05:38.992 --> 00:05:43.960
But when you invest in people early
the return is a network that's

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.230
not just broader but deeper.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.950
And that social network is a competitive
advantage as a leader who wants to get

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:52.720
big things done.

00:05:54.622 --> 00:05:56.993
So, sponsorship.

00:05:56.993 --> 00:05:59.581
It's good for others and
it's good for you.

00:05:59.581 --> 00:06:03.240
But there's a third reason I
want you to consider this.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.630
And that's because sponsorship
is a powerful tool for

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.513
changing organizations
from the inside out.

00:06:09.513 --> 00:06:11.870
No matter what stage you
are at in your own career.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:13.390
And here's why.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:20.214
We know that organizations with diverse
teams have better business outcomes.

00:06:20.214 --> 00:06:24.022
And when I look at the world outside
of our classrooms, I see lots of

00:06:24.022 --> 00:06:28.647
organizations that have figured out how
to hire fairly diverse pools of talent,

00:06:28.647 --> 00:06:30.360
at least at the entry levels.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:33.490
But despite knowing this,

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:38.420
leadership remains looking
stubbornly like it did 30 years ago.

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:43.380
This says to me that
there's something broken

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.609
in how we're moving people
through organization.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:53.544
And that, it looks less like a ladder than
like a climbing wall with the ropes and

00:06:53.544 --> 00:06:57.752
carabiners are only handed
down to some people.

00:06:57.752 --> 00:07:04.054
I believe that in many ways,
it comes down to who is getting sponsored.

00:07:04.054 --> 00:07:10.458
Research shows that we're more likely
to spend time with, communicate with,

00:07:10.458 --> 00:07:16.160
trust, and by extension sponsor,
people who are similar to us.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.710
It's literally human nature.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.090
But in a world where 80% of senior
leaders, and 97% of CEO's are men.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.340
It's perhaps not surprising that
sponsorship can end up looking,

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.470
in some ways, a self-replicating system.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.940
And again, people liking people who are
like them doesn't make them bad people,

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:39.747
just human.

00:07:42.517 --> 00:07:47.440
But what it says is that if we truly
want to create diverse organizations We

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.549
have to do and feels a natural.

00:07:49.549 --> 00:07:51.118
And be intentional.

00:07:51.118 --> 00:07:54.140
About looking for potential and
people who don't look like us.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.060
No matter who are or what you look like.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:05.755
I hope the bunch of people you build,
looks just like this and more like this.

00:08:05.755 --> 00:08:08.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:13.820
I hope that by being explicit about
what sponsorship is, what it's done for

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:17.560
you and me, and
how we can step into that role ourselves,

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:23.745
we could be the ones to break that self
replicating And it's not hard to do.

00:08:23.745 --> 00:08:24.953
First, we can start now.

00:08:24.953 --> 00:08:29.091
So many of us picture our sponsors to be
older and wiser that you don't have to

00:08:29.091 --> 00:08:32.750
wait until you're a shriveled,
green 900 year old to do this.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:35.345
&gt;&gt; [LAUGHS]
&gt;&gt; No matter where you are, there

00:08:35.345 --> 00:08:40.555
are Luke Skywalkers behind you wanting
to go to your academic institution.

00:08:40.555 --> 00:08:43.157
Work for your company or
move up into your current role.

00:08:43.157 --> 00:08:45.484
We can be intentional about looking for

00:08:45.484 --> 00:08:48.820
potential in people who
do not look like us.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:53.250
We can be thoughtful, about giving those
people access to the experiences and

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:56.668
relationships we already have, that they
may not know how to find on their own.

00:08:56.668 --> 00:09:01.381
And finally, we can be strategic.

00:09:01.381 --> 00:09:03.380
About advocating for their promotion,

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:07.270
so that they can benefit from our own
attempts to make it up that climbing wall.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:12.000
To change the world that we inherit,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.350
we need not just more sponsorship,
but better sponsorship.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:19.835
And we need it to start now,
not in another decade.

00:09:19.835 --> 00:09:24.229
So I hope that this talk could
be a kind of playbook, for

00:09:24.229 --> 00:09:29.402
us to go out into the world and
be intentional about sponsoring.

00:09:29.402 --> 00:09:30.551
And I hope you do so,

00:09:30.551 --> 00:09:35.660
because it's a powerful way to have
an impact on somebody else's life.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:37.190
And I hope you do so

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:41.260
because it's an investment in yourself and
your ability to get big things done.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:45.620
And I hope you do so
because it's a powerful tool for

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:47.730
changing organizations
from the inside out.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:54.533
But most of all, I hope you do so
because our legacies are not

00:09:54.533 --> 00:09:59.470
just the businesses and
bank accounts we leave behind.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:05.720
But rather,
the people whose lives we helped to build.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:12.857
You know what it's like to have found
your Yoda So go out and find your look.

